---
/* THE PAGE THE FRONT PAGE KEPT FOLDING AWAY.
 *
 * Two panels of prose — why the atlas is a map, and what it does not know —
 * sat under the map behind a <details>. Folding them was the right call for
 * the front page and the wrong home for the text: a reader who has already
 * got the point scrolled past it every visit, and a reader who arrived with a
 * surname met an essay before they met an answer.
 *
 * So the text lives here, on a page somebody reaches by asking for help, and
 * it is allowed to be longer than a fold. The front page keeps the map.
 *
 * WHILE MOVING IT, TWO THINGS WERE WRONG AND ARE FIXED:
 *   · «Online is four different things» — it is eight, and the eighth is
 *     «nobody has looked», which is nine dots in ten.
 *   · The counts printed as 21473 and 2547650, unseparated, because the front
 *     page interpolated them raw.
 * Every number here is counted at build time.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { atlas as idx } from "../lib/atlas.js";
import regions from "../../public/regions.json";
import provs from "../../public/providers.json";

const places = idx.places.length;
const volumes = idx.places.reduce((a, p) => a + (p.v || 0), 0);
const archives = provs.providers.filter((p) => p.at);
const archiveCountries = new Set(archives.map((p) => p.at.place.split(", ").pop())).size;

const RANK = idx.rank || [];
const SURVEYED = new Set(["free", "account", "index", "mixed", "paid", "catalogue", "onsite"]);
const accessOf = (p) => (typeof p.a === "number" && RANK[p.a]) || "unsurveyed";
const surveyed = idx.places.filter((p) => SURVEYED.has(accessOf(p))).length;
const unsurveyedPct = Math.round((1 - surveyed / places) * 1000) / 10;

/* The access vocabulary, read from the index rather than restated, so this
   page cannot drift from the colours the map actually draws. */
const access = idx.access || {};
---
<Base
  title="Help"
  eyebrow="How to search it · what the colours mean · what to do when it finds nothing"
  dek="Find a place whose name has changed, read the colour of a dot, and know what to do when the answer is nothing.">

  <h2 class="k-h">Four ways to search</h2>
  <ul class="k-tiles hp-tiles">
    <li><b>A surname</b><span>The map lights the countries where a register or archive records it. That is where to start, not proof your family lived there.</span></li>
    <li><b>A village, by any name</b><span>Gallignana goes to Gračišće, Breslau to Wrocław, Memel to Klaipėda. It works worldwide, not only where the shelf is deep.</span></li>
    <li><b>A name and a place</b><span><em>Blazevic Gracisce</em> narrows to that name on that ground.</span></li>
    <li><b>Click a country</b><span>Every country answers with something: whose national archive, what research guide exists.</span></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="k-h">What the colour of a dot means <span class="k-count">what it costs to look</span></h2>
  <ul class="k-tiles hp-tiles hp-key">
    {Object.entries(access).map(([k, v]) => (
      <li><b>{k}</b><span>{v}</span></li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p class="k-lede">“Online” is eight different things, not one. The last of these is the commonest colour on the map.
    Set a year and the map also says whose archive held that ground: <a href={u("/jurisdictions/")}>the table behind that</a>.</p>

  <h2 class="k-h">When it finds nothing</h2>
  <div class="k-hi"><p><b>A grey dot means nobody has surveyed it, not that nothing is there.</b></p></div>
  <ol class="k-steps">
    <li><b>Set the year.</b> See whose archive held that ground. The records may be filed under a country you were not searching.</li>
    <li><b>Open the source directory</b> for that country and write to the archive named there. <a href={u("/providers/")}>The sources →</a></li>
    <li><b>Try the newspapers.</b> For some countries, Australia above all, a newspaper is the record.</li>
    <li><b>Found it?</b> <a href={u("/add/")}>Send it back.</a> A grey dot you turned into an answer is the most valuable thing a stranger can give this map.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 class="k-h">What it does not know yet</h2>
  <div class="k-stats">
    <span class="k-tag is-new">{unsurveyedPct}% never surveyed</span>
    <span><b>{archives.length}</b> archives in <b>{archiveCountries}</b> countries</span>
    <span><b>{places.toLocaleString()}</b> places</span>
    <span><b>{volumes.toLocaleString()}</b> volumes</span>
    <span><b>{regions.regions.length}</b> jurisdiction regions</span>
  </div>
  <p class="k-lede">The shelf is deep in one corner: Croatia, Istria and Carnia are far ahead. The jurisdiction layer covers
    {regions.regions.length} pieces of ground, not the planet, and a place outside them says so rather than guessing. A confident wrong answer about which archive to write to costs somebody a month.</p>

  <div class="k-foot">
    <p>Still stuck?</p>
    <p><a href={u("/start/")}>Start here</a> · <a href={u("/about/")}>About</a> · <a href={u("/coverage/")}>What’s missing</a></p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .hp-tiles { grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); max-width: 980px; }
  .hp-tiles li > span { display: block; }
  .hp-tiles li b { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 17px; font-weight: 500; color: var(--ink); }
  .hp-key li b { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11.5px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em; }
</style>
